{#
  End-of-body scripts and structured data.

  Included from _layouts/page.twig for EVERY page, so anything added here has to
  say which pages it is for. Right now that's one thing: JSON-LD, on the three
  page types that have something worth describing.

  JSON-LD in the body rather than the head is deliberate and supported —
  crawlers read `application/ld+json` wherever it sits, and down here it costs
  the first paint nothing. (It is data, not a script: nothing executes it.)

  WHAT EACH PAGE DESCRIBES

  Every page carries the two nodes that don't change — the site and the person
  whose site it is — and then whatever its own type warrants:

    /                     ProfilePage   → the Person is the subject of the page,
                          named and described from the entry's SEO tab
    /case-studies         CollectionPage + ItemList of the studies, in the order
                          the page shows them
    /case-study/{slug}    WebPage (named and described from the entry's SEO tab,
                          heading and Bio as fallbacks, the OG image as its image)
                          + CreativeWork (the work), Organization (the client),
                          Review (its testimonials)
    /contact              ContactPage   → the Person is who you'd be contacting,
                          named and described from the entry's SEO tab
    /about                AboutPage     → the Person again, named and described
                          from the entry's SEO tab (title, meta description, OG
                          image) with the h1 and Summary as fallbacks
    /{slug}               WebPage       → a standing page (a policy, a statement),
                          named and described from its SEO tab
    /notes                CollectionPage + ItemList of the notes on THIS page
    /notes/{topic}        of the listing (six a page, newest first), each a
    /notes/{year}         reference to the note's own BlogPosting node. The
                          topic and year pages aren't entries — one is a
                          category, the other a route — so they're picked out
                          by what the view hands the layout, not by section
    /notes/{slug}         WebPage + BlogPosting (the note: headline, dates, its
                          pictures), by the Person, published by the site

  Everything but the homepage also carries a BreadcrumbList. The homepage is the
  root, and a one-item trail says nothing.

  ONE <script> PER TYPE, tagged `data-schema="Review"` and so on, so a given
  block can be found and read on its own rather than by scrolling one long blob.
  Each block is a standalone document: it carries its own @context, and a type
  with several nodes (Review, on a study with more than one quote) wraps them in
  a @graph.

  The cross-block @id references rely on the consumer merging every ld+json
  block on the page into one dataset before it resolves them. Google, Bing and
  the schema.org validator all do. A strict single-document JSON-LD processor
  would not, and would see those as unresolved references. That's the price of
  the split; put everything back in one @graph if you ever need to pay it back.

  Every value comes from the CMS or from config. Nothing here is written twice:
  the same `longTitle ?: title` fallback the views and FindContext use resolves
  a study's name, a study's description is the entry's Bio (the field this entry
  type uses for what other sections call `summary` — see FindContext), and the
  profile URLs are the list the footer icons are built from.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT

  · SearchAction on the homepage. The ask bar looks like a GET form, but
    ask-handoff.js intercepts it and hands the question over in sessionStorage —
    `/?question=…` is not a URL that does anything. A potentialAction pointing
    at it would send crawlers somewhere that doesn't answer.
  · VideoObject for a caseContent video block — we have the clip, the poster and
    a date, but not a name or description of its own, and Google wants both.
#}
{% set schemaEntry = entry is defined and entry ? entry : null %}
{% set schemaSection = schemaEntry ? schemaEntry.section.handle : null %}

{# The notes listings. Three pages share one listing (_components/notes-index)
   and one shape of structured data, but only one of them is an entry: /notes is
   the `note` single, /notes/{topic} is a category in the Topics group, and
   /notes/{year} is a route (config/routes.php) that hands the view a `year`.
   Each is recognised by what it hands the layout, and reduced here to the
   handful of facts the listing branch needs — where it lives, what it's called,
   its standfirst, and the crumb it adds after "Notes". Null on every other page.

   The notes index entry is looked up for the trail: its URL and its section's
   name are what the topic and year pages show as their eyebrow and what a
   crawler should see as the parent. #}
{% set listing = null %}
{% if schemaSection == 'note' or (category is defined and category and category.group.handle == 'topics') or (year is defined and year and notesQuery is defined) %}
    {% set notesIndex = schemaSection == 'note' ? schemaEntry : craft.entries().section('note').status('live').one() %}
    {% set notesUrl = notesIndex ? notesIndex.url : siteUrl('notes') %}
    {% set notesName = notesIndex ? notesIndex.section.name : 'Notes' %}

    {% if schemaSection == 'note' %}
        {% set indexHeading = (schemaEntry.longTitle ?? '')|trim ?: schemaEntry.title|trim %}
        {% set listing = {
            url: schemaEntry.url,
            name: (schemaEntry.seoCustomPageTitle ?? '')|trim ?: indexHeading,
            heading: indexHeading,
            description: (schemaEntry.seoMetaDescription ?? '')|trim ?: (schemaEntry.summary ?? ''),
            share: schemaEntry,
            modified: schemaEntry.dateUpdated,
            crumbs: [],
        } %}
    {% elseif category is defined and category %}
        {# The SEO tab first, read the way the head reads it: Custom Page Title,
           else the h1; Meta Description, else the topic's Summary. `heading` keeps
           the h1 whatever the page is titled for search. #}
        {% set topicHeading = (category.longTitle ?? '')|trim ?: category.title|trim %}
        {% set listing = {
            url: category.url,
            name: (category.seoCustomPageTitle ?? '')|trim ?: topicHeading,
            heading: topicHeading,
            description: (category.seoMetaDescription ?? '')|trim ?: (category.summary ?? ''),
            share: category,
            modified: category.dateUpdated,
            crumbs: [{ name: category.title|trim, item: category.url }],
        } %}
    {% else %}
        {# `share: null` — a year archive is a route, not an element, so there is
           nothing to read a share image off. (Said here, not in the hash: a Twig
           comment inside a hash literal is a parse error that 500s the page.) #}
        {% set listing = {
            url: notesUrl ~ '/' ~ year,
            name: year ~ '',
            heading: year ~ '',
            description: '',
            share: null,
            modified: null,
            crumbs: [{ name: year ~ '', item: notesUrl ~ '/' ~ year }],
        } %}
    {% endif %}
{% endif %}

{% if listing or schemaSection in ['homepage', 'caseStudy', 'caseStudies', 'contact', 'about', 'pages', 'notes', 'tools'] %}
    {% set globals = craft.entries().section('globals').one() %}
    {% set strapline = globals ? (globals.strapline ?? '')|trim : '' %}
    {% set siteName = craft.app.sites.currentSite.name %}
    {% set language = craft.app.sites.currentSite.language %}

    {% set homeUrl = siteUrl('') %}
    {% set indexUrl = siteUrl('case-studies') %}
    {% set personId = homeUrl ~ '#person' %}
    {% set websiteId = homeUrl ~ '#website' %}
    {% set pageId = schemaEntry ? schemaEntry.url ~ '#webpage' : null %}

    {# `sameAs` — the profiles that identify this Jon Leverrier as opposed to any
       other. That's the whole job of the property: an identity a search engine
       or an AI system can reconcile against a profile it already knows.

       Straight from craft/config/social.php, the same list the footer icons are
       built from, so the schema can't claim a profile the page doesn't link to.
       Null when the list is empty, so the key drops out rather than emitting [].

       (Set out here, not inline — a Twig comment inside a hash literal is a
       parse error that takes the whole page down with it. Same for every other
       hoisted value below.) #}
    {% set sameAs = craft.app.config.getConfigFromFile('social')|map(l => l.url)|values %}
    {% set sameAs = sameAs|length ? sameAs : null %}

    {# Every trail starts at Home; each page appends its own steps below. #}
    {% set crumbs = [
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: homeUrl },
    ] %}

    {# Email for the Person, read out of the globals single's CTAs — the
       `mailto:` link the contact page already renders. Read rather than
       retyped, so the schema can only ever offer a route the site itself
       offers: change that CTA and this follows, remove it and the key drops
       out.

       It lands on the shared Person node, not on the contact page alone. It's
       one @id describing one man, and a node that said different things on
       different pages is a node a merging consumer has to pick between.

       The `tel:` CTA is deliberately NOT read across. It's a personal mobile,
       and structured data is the first format a harvester parses — putting it
       on every page of the site is a different exposure from one anchor tag on
       /contact, for no gain a visitor ever sees. The email is a work address
       and answering strangers is the point of it. #}
    {% set contactUrls = globals
        ? globals.ctas.all()|map(c => craft.frontend.safeUrl(c.ctaUrl.url ?? ''))|filter(u => u)|values
        : [] %}
    {% set mailtos = contactUrls|filter(u => u starts with 'mailto:')|values %}
    {% set email = mailtos|length ? mailtos|first|slice(7) : null %}

    {# What the About page knows about the Person, read on EVERY page: the node
       is one @id describing one man, so it has to say the same thing wherever
       it appears, and the About page is where those facts live.

       jobTitle is the first line of the header's role list (the Global entry's Job
       Titles field, via craft.frontend.roles()) — the same source, so the schema can't
       claim a title the site doesn't show.
       knowsAbout is the method timeline's service names, the things the site says
       he does, deduplicated. image is the About page's first large image, as an
       original asset URL (a transform URL would have Craft render a file no
       visitor loads). Each drops out when its source is empty. #}
    {% set roles = craft.frontend.roles() %}
    {% set jobTitle = roles|length ? roles|first : null %}

    {% set knowsAbout = [] %}
    {% for phase in craft.frontend.methodology() %}
        {% set knowsAbout = knowsAbout|merge(phase.services) %}
    {% endfor %}
    {% set knowsAbout = knowsAbout|unique|values %}
    {% set knowsAbout = knowsAbout|length ? knowsAbout : null %}

    {% set aboutEntry = craft.entries().section('about').status('live').one() %}
    {% set portrait = null %}
    {% if aboutEntry %}
        {% for block in aboutEntry.caseContent.all() %}
            {% if portrait is null and block.type.handle == 'largeImage' and block.largeImage.one() %}
                {% set portrait = block.largeImage.one() %}
            {% endif %}
        {% endfor %}
    {% endif %}
    {% set portraitImage = portrait ? {
        '@type': 'ImageObject',
        url: portrait.url,
        width: portrait.width,
        height: portrait.height,
    }|filter(v => v) : null %}

    {% set schema = {} %}

    {#  ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
        THE NOTES LISTINGS — /notes, /notes/{topic}, /notes/{year} (and /p2 …)
        ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— #}
    {% if listing %}
        {# Same shape as the case study index — CollectionPage framing an
           ItemList — with one difference: these lists are paginated, so each
           describes the notes on THIS page, not the whole set. Page 3 lists
           different notes from page 1 and is canonical at its own /p3 address
           (see head.twig), so its structured data says what that page shows, at
           that URL.

           The query is the view's own `notesQuery` — set at its top level, so
           the layout sees it, the same way the head reads `pageCount` — cloned
           before the offset is applied so the view's query isn't touched, and
           offset by the request's page number the way `paginate` does it. If a
           listing ever renders through this layout without setting the query,
           the list is empty and the page still gets its CollectionPage. #}
        {% set pageNum = craft.app.request.pageNum %}
        {% set pageTrigger = craft.app.config.general.pageTrigger ?? 'p' %}
        {% set pageUrl = pageNum > 1 ? listing.url ~ '/' ~ pageTrigger ~ pageNum : listing.url %}
        {% set pageId = pageUrl ~ '#webpage' %}
        {% set listId = pageUrl ~ '#notes' %}
        {% set pageSize = perPage ?? 6 %}

        {# The Open Graph image at the 1200×630 the og:image tag already rendered, so
           no extra transform, with the site-wide fallback the head uses. Null on a
           year archive, which is a route rather than an element. #}
        {% set shareAsset = listing.share ? craft.frontend.socialImage(listing.share) : null %}
        {% set shareImage = shareAsset ? {
            '@type': 'ImageObject',
            url: craft.frontend.socialImageUrl(shareAsset),
            width: 1200,
            height: 630,
            caption: (shareAsset.alt ?? '')|trim,
        }|filter(v => v) : null %}

        {% set pageNotes = notesQuery is defined and notesQuery
            ? clone(notesQuery).offset((pageNum - 1) * pageSize).all()
            : [] %}

        {# Each item points at the note's own BlogPosting node by @id — the one
           its page defines — and carries the headline and URL inline so this
           list still reads on its own. Positions count from where this page
           starts in the whole listing, so a consumer that sees page 2 knows it's
           looking at items seven to twelve, not one to six again. #}
        {% set items = [] %}
        {% for n in pageNotes %}
            {% set items = items|merge([{
                '@type': 'ListItem',
                position: (pageNum - 1) * pageSize + loop.index,
                item: {
                    '@type': 'BlogPosting',
                    '@id': n.url ~ '#post',
                    headline: n.title|trim,
                    url: n.url,
                    datePublished: n.postDate ? n.postDate|atom : null,
                }|filter(v => v),
            }]) %}
        {% endfor %}

        {# The standfirst is the page's Summary, a plain-text field with blank
           lines as paragraph breaks — one description, so the breaks collapse to
           spaces. The year page has none. #}
        {% set summary = listing.description|replace({"\n": ' '})|trim %}
        {% set summary = summary|split(' ')|filter(w => w)|join(' ') %}

        {# The trail: Home, Notes, then the topic or year if this is one. #}
        {% set trail = [{ '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: notesName, item: notesUrl }] %}
        {% for c in listing.crumbs %}
            {% set trail = trail|merge([{ '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2 + loop.index, name: c.name, item: c.item }]) %}
        {% endfor %}

        {% set schema = {
            CollectionPage: [{
                '@type': 'CollectionPage',
                '@id': pageId,
                url: pageUrl,
                name: pageNum > 1 ? listing.name ~ ' — page ' ~ pageNum : listing.name,
                headline: listing.heading,
                image: shareImage,
                description: summary,
                inLanguage: language,
                isPartOf: { '@id': websiteId },
                author: { '@id': personId },
                mainEntity: { '@id': listId },
                breadcrumb: { '@id': pageId ~ '-breadcrumb' },
                dateModified: listing.modified ? listing.modified|atom : null,
            }|filter(v => v)],
            ItemList: [{
                '@type': 'ItemList',
                '@id': listId,
                name: listing.heading,
                itemListOrder: 'https://schema.org/ItemListOrderDescending',
                numberOfItems: items|length,
                itemListElement: items,
            }|filter(v => v)],
            BreadcrumbList: [{
                '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
                '@id': pageId ~ '-breadcrumb',
                itemListElement: crumbs|merge(trail),
            }],
        } %}

    {#  ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
        A SINGLE CASE STUDY — /case-study/{slug}
        ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— #}
    {% elseif schemaSection == 'caseStudies' %}
        {% set workId = schemaEntry.url ~ '#casestudy' %}
        {% set clientId = schemaEntry.url ~ '#client' %}

        {# One display name per project, resolved the way every other surface
           resolves it — the card, the page heading and the model's background
           list all land on the same string. Both names are the STUDY's own; the
           client is a relation, and the long form is right here because the
           Organization node below is the client itself, not a passing mention. #}
        {% set heading = (schemaEntry.longTitle ?? '')|trim ?: schemaEntry.title|trim %}
        {% set client = craft.frontend.caseStudyClientName(schemaEntry, true) %}

        {# The entry's SEO tab first, read the way the head reads it so the two can't
           disagree: the Meta Description when set, else Bio (the public one-line
           blurb on this entry type), else Case Summary — CKEditor HTML, so it's
           stripped back to text and capped rather than passed through; a
           description is a sentence, not a document. #}
        {% set summary = (schemaEntry.seoMetaDescription ?? '')|trim ?: (schemaEntry.bio ?? '')|trim %}
        {% if not summary %}
            {% set summary = (schemaEntry.caseSummary ?? '')|striptags|trim %}
            {% set summary = summary|length > 300 ? summary|slice(0, 297)|trim ~ '…' : summary %}
        {% endif %}

        {# The page's name is the Custom Page Title when set (what the head puts in
           <title>), else the heading; the heading stays as `headline`. The WORK keeps
           the heading as its name either way — a study is called what it's called,
           whatever the page is titled for search. The share image is the Open Graph
           one at the 1200×630 the og:image tag already rendered (no extra transform),
           with the site-wide fallback the head uses; primaryImageOfPage stays the
           first picture in the study itself. #}
        {% set seoTitle = (schemaEntry.seoCustomPageTitle ?? '')|trim %}
        {% set shareAsset = craft.frontend.socialImage(schemaEntry) %}
        {% set shareImage = shareAsset ? {
            '@type': 'ImageObject',
            url: craft.frontend.socialImageUrl(shareAsset),
            width: 1200,
            height: 630,
            caption: (shareAsset.alt ?? '')|trim,
        }|filter(v => v) : null %}

        {# Skills and sectors are the same categories the page and the model
           read. Merged into one keyword list: what the work involved and the
           market it was for are both terms someone would search this study by. #}
        {% set keywords = schemaEntry.skills.all()|map(c => c.title)
            |merge(schemaEntry.sectorSelector.all()|map(c => c.title))|values %}

        {# Post Date carries the year the project ran, not the publish day (see
           the case study view). The "Date hidden?" switch drops the year from
           the page for work old enough that dating it would undersell it — so
           it drops out of the structured data too, rather than quietly telling
           a crawler what the page declines to tell a visitor. #}
        {% set published = (schemaEntry.hasDateHidden ?? false) or not schemaEntry.postDate
            ? null
            : schemaEntry.postDate|atom %}
        {% set modified = schemaEntry.dateUpdated ? schemaEntry.dateUpdated|atom : null %}

        {# The entry's Thumbnail first, then the Case Content imagery in author
           order — the same assets the page shows, so what a crawler is told the
           page contains is what it contains.

           Branched on `block.type.handle` rather than testing whether a field
           is there: a Matrix block only carries its own type's fields, and
           reading a field a block type doesn't have throws.

           Original asset URLs, not transforms. A transform URL here would have
           Craft generate an image on page render that no visitor ever loads. #}
        {% set assets = [schemaEntry.largeImage.one()] %}
        {% for block in schemaEntry.caseContent.all() %}
            {% if block.type.handle in ['largeImage', 'video'] %}
                {% set assets = assets|merge([block.largeImage.one()]) %}
            {% elseif block.type.handle == 'twoUpImage' %}
                {% set assets = assets|merge([
                    block.twoUpImagePrimary.one(),
                    block.twoUpImageSecondary.one(),
                ]) %}
            {% endif %}
        {% endfor %}

        {% set images = [] %}
        {% for asset in assets|filter(a => a) %}
            {% set images = images|merge([{
                '@type': 'ImageObject',
                url: asset.url,
                width: asset.width,
                height: asset.height,
            }|filter(v => v)]) %}
        {% endfor %}

        {# The client's logo, falling back to the study's own — the same order the
           cards use, so the Organization's logo is the one on the page. #}
        {% set logo = craft.frontend.caseStudyClientLogo(schemaEntry) %}

        {# This study's testimonials — the ones PLACED on the page, as
           caseTestimonial blocks in the Case Content, in page order. Placement is
           the switch for a quote on a study (the view dropped the relation-driven
           slider; see _views/structure/caseStudies), so the structured data reads
           the same blocks the visitor reads and can never claim a review the page
           doesn't show, or miss one it does. The Case Study Link relation still
           says WHOSE quote it is — that's the author's Organization below — it
           just no longer decides which quotes are here. A quote placed twice is
           one Review.

           `reviewBody` is plain text: the field is CKEditor, and schema.org
           wants the words, not the markup wrapping them.

           The author works for the client Organization already in the graph, so
           `worksFor` points at that @id instead of restating the name.

           No `reviewRating`: there's no rating field on a testimonial, and
           inventing five stars nobody gave is exactly the kind of thing
           structured data gets penalised for. That makes these ineligible for a
           review rich result but perfectly valid as review data. Add a rating
           field and they qualify.

           No `datePublished` either. The only date a testimonial carries is its
           entry's Post Date — the day the quote was added to the CMS, which is
           not the day it was given. Close enough to look right and wrong often
           enough to mislead, so it's better left unsaid than approximated. #}
        {% set reviews = [] %}
        {% set reviewed = [] %}
        {% for block in schemaEntry.caseContent.all()|filter(b => b.type.handle == 'caseTestimonial') %}
            {% set t = block.testimonial.one() %}
            {% set body = t ? (t.blockquote ?? '')|striptags|trim : '' %}
            {% if body and t.id not in reviewed %}
                {% set reviewed = reviewed|merge([t.id]) %}
                {% set reviews = reviews|merge([{
                    '@type': 'Review',
                    '@id': schemaEntry.url ~ '#review-' ~ t.id,
                    itemReviewed: { '@id': workId },
                    reviewBody: body,
                    author: {
                        '@type': 'Person',
                        name: (t.personName ?? '')|trim,
                        jobTitle: (t.jobTitle ?? '')|trim,
                        worksFor: client ? { '@id': clientId } : null,
                    }|filter(v => v),
                }|filter(v => v)]) %}
            {% endif %}
        {% endfor %}

        {# CreativeWork points at the quotes by @id rather than nesting them:
           they are their own block, and a node can only be spelled out in one
           place without the two copies drifting. #}
        {% set reviewRefs = reviews|length ? reviews|map(r => { '@id': r['@id'] })|values : null %}

        {% set schema = {
            WebPage: [{
                '@type': 'WebPage',
                '@id': pageId,
                url: schemaEntry.url,
                name: seoTitle ?: heading,
                headline: heading,
                description: summary,
                inLanguage: language,
                isPartOf: { '@id': websiteId },
                mainEntity: { '@id': workId },
                breadcrumb: { '@id': pageId ~ '-breadcrumb' },
                primaryImageOfPage: images|length ? images|first : null,
                image: shareImage,
                datePublished: published,
                dateModified: modified,
            }|filter(v => v)],
            CreativeWork: [{
                '@type': 'CreativeWork',
                '@id': workId,
                url: schemaEntry.url,
                name: heading,
                headline: heading,
                description: summary,
                inLanguage: language,
                author: { '@id': personId },
                creator: { '@id': personId },
                about: client ? { '@id': clientId } : null,
                keywords: keywords|length ? keywords : null,
                image: images|length ? images : null,
                review: reviewRefs,
                datePublished: published,
                dateModified: modified,
                isPartOf: { '@id': pageId },
            }|filter(v => v)],
            Organization: client ? [{
                '@type': 'Organization',
                '@id': clientId,
                name: client,
                logo: logo ? {
                    '@type': 'ImageObject',
                    url: logo.url,
                    width: logo.width,
                    height: logo.height,
                }|filter(v => v) : null,
            }|filter(v => v)] : null,
            Review: reviews,
            BreadcrumbList: [{
                '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
                '@id': pageId ~ '-breadcrumb',
                itemListElement: crumbs|merge([
                    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Case Studies', item: indexUrl },
                    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: heading, item: schemaEntry.url },
                ]),
            }],
        } %}

    {#  ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
        THE CASE STUDY INDEX — /case-studies
        ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— #}
    {% elseif schemaSection == 'caseStudy' %}
        {% set listId = schemaEntry.url ~ '#studies' %}
        {% set heading = (schemaEntry.longTitle ?? '')|trim ?: schemaEntry.title|trim %}

        {# The SAME query and ordering as the view — live only, structure order. An
           ItemList that doesn't match the order on the page is describing a page that
           doesn't exist, so this moved when the view stopped promoting the featured
           ones. #}
        {% set ordered = craft.entries().section('caseStudies').status('live').all() %}

        {# Each item links to the study's own CreativeWork node by @id — the one
           that page defines — and carries its name inline so this list still
           says something on its own. #}
        {% set items = [] %}
        {% for s in ordered %}
            {% set items = items|merge([{
                '@type': 'ListItem',
                position: loop.index,
                item: {
                    '@type': 'CreativeWork',
                    '@id': s.url ~ '#casestudy',
                    name: (s.longTitle ?? '')|trim ?: s.title|trim,
                    url: s.url,
                },
            }]) %}
        {% endfor %}

        {% set schema = {
            CollectionPage: [{
                '@type': 'CollectionPage',
                '@id': pageId,
                url: schemaEntry.url,
                name: heading,
                description: (schemaEntry.summary ?? '')|trim,
                inLanguage: language,
                isPartOf: { '@id': websiteId },
                about: { '@id': personId },
                mainEntity: { '@id': listId },
                breadcrumb: { '@id': pageId ~ '-breadcrumb' },
                dateModified: schemaEntry.dateUpdated ? schemaEntry.dateUpdated|atom : null,
            }|filter(v => v)],
            ItemList: [{
                '@type': 'ItemList',
                '@id': listId,
                name: heading,
                numberOfItems: items|length,
                itemListElement: items,
            }|filter(v => v)],
            BreadcrumbList: [{
                '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
                '@id': pageId ~ '-breadcrumb',
                itemListElement: crumbs|merge([
                    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Case Studies', item: indexUrl },
                ]),
            }],
        } %}

    {#  ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
        CONTACT — /contact
        ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— #}
    {% elseif schemaSection == 'contact' %}
        {# ContactPage, the WebPage subtype that says what this page is FOR. The
           person you'd be contacting is the mainEntity; the ways to do it are
           already on the Person node (email, telephone, sameAs), so there's
           nothing to restate here.

           Named and described from the entry's SEO tab, read the way the head
           reads it: Custom Page Title, else the h1 (which stays as `headline`);
           Meta Description when there is one — this single has no Summary field,
           so there's nothing else to fall back to and the key simply drops out.
           The share image is the Open Graph one at the 1200×630 the og:image tag
           already rendered, with the site-wide fallback the head uses. #}
        {% set heading = (schemaEntry.longTitle ?? '')|trim ?: schemaEntry.title|trim %}
        {% set seoTitle = (schemaEntry.seoCustomPageTitle ?? '')|trim %}
        {% set shareAsset = craft.frontend.socialImage(schemaEntry) %}
        {% set shareImage = shareAsset ? {
            '@type': 'ImageObject',
            url: craft.frontend.socialImageUrl(shareAsset),
            width: 1200,
            height: 630,
            caption: (shareAsset.alt ?? '')|trim,
        }|filter(v => v) : null %}

        {% set schema = {
            ContactPage: [{
                '@type': 'ContactPage',
                '@id': pageId,
                url: schemaEntry.url,
                name: seoTitle ?: heading,
                headline: heading,
                description: (schemaEntry.seoMetaDescription ?? '')|trim,
                inLanguage: language,
                isPartOf: { '@id': websiteId },
                mainEntity: { '@id': personId },
                image: shareImage,
                breadcrumb: { '@id': pageId ~ '-breadcrumb' },
                dateModified: schemaEntry.dateUpdated ? schemaEntry.dateUpdated|atom : null,
            }|filter(v => v)],
            BreadcrumbList: [{
                '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
                '@id': pageId ~ '-breadcrumb',
                itemListElement: crumbs|merge([
                    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Contact', item: schemaEntry.url },
                ]),
            }],
        } %}

    {#  ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
        A NOTE — /notes/{slug}
        ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— #}
    {% elseif schemaSection == 'notes' %}
        {# BlogPosting, the Article subtype for a personal post, as the page's
           mainEntity; the WebPage frames it, as on a case study. Headline is the
           h1 (`longTitle ?: title`); the breadcrumb keeps the short title, as a
           case study's does. The description is the Summary the page opens with,
           falling back to the Meta Description. Dates are the entry's own —
           postDate is what the CMS calls the published date. Pictures are the
           note's imagery blocks, in page order, as original asset URLs (a
           transform URL here would have Craft render a file no visitor loads). #}
        {% set postId = schemaEntry.url ~ '#post' %}
        {% set headline = (schemaEntry.longTitle ?? '')|trim ?: schemaEntry.title|trim %}
        {% set summary = (schemaEntry.summary ?? '')|striptags|trim
            ?: (schemaEntry.seoMetaDescription ?? '')|striptags|trim %}
        {% set published = schemaEntry.postDate ? schemaEntry.postDate|atom : null %}
        {% set modified = schemaEntry.dateUpdated ? schemaEntry.dateUpdated|atom : null %}

        {% set assets = [] %}
        {% for block in schemaEntry.caseContent.all() %}
            {% if block.type.handle in ['largeImage', 'video'] %}
                {% set assets = assets|merge([block.largeImage.one()]) %}
            {% elseif block.type.handle == 'legacyImage' %}
                {% set assets = assets|merge([block.legacyImage.one()]) %}
            {% elseif block.type.handle == 'twoUpImage' %}
                {% set assets = assets|merge([
                    block.twoUpImagePrimary.one(),
                    block.twoUpImageSecondary.one(),
                ]) %}
            {% endif %}
        {% endfor %}
        {% set images = [] %}
        {% for asset in assets|filter(a => a) %}
            {% set images = images|merge([{
                '@type': 'ImageObject',
                url: asset.url,
                width: asset.width,
                height: asset.height,
            }|filter(v => v)]) %}
        {% endfor %}

        {% set schema = {
            WebPage: [{
                '@type': 'WebPage',
                '@id': pageId,
                url: schemaEntry.url,
                name: headline,
                description: summary,
                inLanguage: language,
                isPartOf: { '@id': websiteId },
                mainEntity: { '@id': postId },
                breadcrumb: { '@id': pageId ~ '-breadcrumb' },
                primaryImageOfPage: images|length ? images|first : null,
                datePublished: published,
                dateModified: modified,
            }|filter(v => v)],
            BlogPosting: [{
                '@type': 'BlogPosting',
                '@id': postId,
                url: schemaEntry.url,
                headline: headline,
                description: summary,
                inLanguage: language,
                author: { '@id': personId },
                publisher: { '@id': personId },
                image: images|length ? images : null,
                datePublished: published,
                dateModified: modified,
                mainEntityOfPage: { '@id': pageId },
                isPartOf: { '@id': websiteId },
            }|filter(v => v)],
            BreadcrumbList: [{
                '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
                '@id': pageId ~ '-breadcrumb',
                itemListElement: crumbs|merge([
                    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: schemaEntry.section.name, item: siteUrl('notes') },
                    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: schemaEntry.title|trim, item: schemaEntry.url },
                ]),
            }],
        } %}

    {#  ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
        ABOUT — /about
        ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— #}
    {% elseif schemaSection == 'about' %}
        {# AboutPage: the WebPage subtype for a page about the site's subject. The
           Person is the mainEntity, and the entry's SEO tab is read the same way
           the head reads it, so the structured data and the <title>/meta tags can
           never disagree:
             · name        — the Custom Page Title when set (what the head puts in
                             <title>), else the h1; the h1 stays as `headline`.
             · description — the Meta Description when set, else the Summary (the
                             standfirst) — the head's own fallback order.
             · image       — the Open Graph image, at the 1200×630 the og:image tag
                             already had rendered (so no extra transform), falling
                             back to the site-wide one exactly as the head does.
           primaryImageOfPage stays the portrait the Person node carries. #}
        {% set heading = (schemaEntry.longTitle ?? '')|trim ?: schemaEntry.title|trim %}
        {% set seoTitle = (schemaEntry.seoCustomPageTitle ?? '')|trim %}
        {% set seoDescription = (schemaEntry.seoMetaDescription ?? '')|trim %}
        {% set shareAsset = craft.frontend.socialImage(schemaEntry) %}
        {% set shareImage = shareAsset ? {
            '@type': 'ImageObject',
            url: craft.frontend.socialImageUrl(shareAsset),
            width: 1200,
            height: 630,
            caption: (shareAsset.alt ?? '')|trim,
        }|filter(v => v) : null %}

        {% set schema = {
            AboutPage: [{
                '@type': 'AboutPage',
                '@id': pageId,
                url: schemaEntry.url,
                name: seoTitle ?: heading,
                headline: heading,
                description: seoDescription ?: (schemaEntry.summary ?? '')|trim,
                inLanguage: language,
                isPartOf: { '@id': websiteId },
                mainEntity: { '@id': personId },
                about: { '@id': personId },
                primaryImageOfPage: portraitImage,
                image: shareImage,
                breadcrumb: { '@id': pageId ~ '-breadcrumb' },
                dateModified: schemaEntry.dateUpdated ? schemaEntry.dateUpdated|atom : null,
            }|filter(v => v)],
            BreadcrumbList: [{
                '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
                '@id': pageId ~ '-breadcrumb',
                itemListElement: crumbs|merge([
                    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: schemaEntry.title|trim, item: schemaEntry.url },
                ]),
            }],
        } %}

    {#  ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
        A PLAIN PAGE — /{slug}  (AI Policy, Privacy Policy, …)
        ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— #}
    {% elseif schemaSection == 'pages' %}
        {# WebPage and nothing more. These are the site's own standing documents —
           a policy, a statement — so there is no work, person or list to hang off
           them; the shared WebSite and Person nodes below say whose site it is.
           Named and described from the SEO tab, read the way the head reads it so
           the two can't disagree: Custom Page Title, else the h1 (which stays as
           `headline`); Meta Description, else the page's Summary. The share image
           is the Open Graph one at the 1200×630 the og:image tag already rendered,
           with the site-wide fallback the head uses. #}
        {% set heading = (schemaEntry.longTitle ?? '')|trim ?: schemaEntry.title|trim %}
        {% set seoTitle = (schemaEntry.seoCustomPageTitle ?? '')|trim %}
        {% set seoDescription = (schemaEntry.seoMetaDescription ?? '')|trim %}
        {% set shareAsset = craft.frontend.socialImage(schemaEntry) %}
        {% set shareImage = shareAsset ? {
            '@type': 'ImageObject',
            url: craft.frontend.socialImageUrl(shareAsset),
            width: 1200,
            height: 630,
            caption: (shareAsset.alt ?? '')|trim,
        }|filter(v => v) : null %}

        {% set schema = {
            WebPage: [{
                '@type': 'WebPage',
                '@id': pageId,
                url: schemaEntry.url,
                name: seoTitle ?: heading,
                headline: heading,
                description: seoDescription ?: (schemaEntry.summary ?? '')|trim,
                inLanguage: language,
                isPartOf: { '@id': websiteId },
                about: { '@id': personId },
                image: shareImage,
                breadcrumb: { '@id': pageId ~ '-breadcrumb' },
                dateModified: schemaEntry.dateUpdated ? schemaEntry.dateUpdated|atom : null,
            }|filter(v => v)],
            BreadcrumbList: [{
                '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
                '@id': pageId ~ '-breadcrumb',
                itemListElement: crumbs|merge([
                    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: schemaEntry.title|trim, item: schemaEntry.url },
                ]),
            }],
        } %}

    {% elseif schemaSection == 'tools' %}
        {# WebPage plus the thing the page actually offers. A tool page is not a
           document like /pages — it is a service somebody can have, free, and the
           Service + Offer pair is what says so to a machine.

           BE CLEAR WHAT THIS BUYS. Google renders no snippet for Service or for a
           zero-price Offer, so nothing new appears in a search result; the only
           visibly rich node here is the BreadcrumbList. What it does buy is that
           the page joins the graph — provider points at the same #person the rest
           of the site declares — so a reader that already knows who Jon is can tell
           this offer is his. That matters more than usual on this site, which
           publishes /llms.txt and runs an assistant for exactly those readers.

           NO AggregateRating, and no Product. Either would draw stars in a result
           and both would be invented: nobody has rated this. Named and described
           from the SEO tab, read the way the head reads it, as every branch above. #}
        {% set heading = (schemaEntry.longTitle ?? '')|trim ?: schemaEntry.title|trim %}
        {% set seoTitle = (schemaEntry.seoCustomPageTitle ?? '')|trim %}
        {% set seoDescription = (schemaEntry.seoMetaDescription ?? '')|trim %}
        {% set toolSummary = seoDescription ?: (schemaEntry.summary ?? '')|trim %}
        {% set serviceId = pageId ~ '-service' %}
        {% set shareAsset = craft.frontend.socialImage(schemaEntry) %}
        {% set shareImage = shareAsset ? {
            '@type': 'ImageObject',
            url: craft.frontend.socialImageUrl(shareAsset),
            width: 1200,
            height: 630,
            caption: (shareAsset.alt ?? '')|trim,
        }|filter(v => v) : null %}

        {% set schema = {
            WebPage: [{
                '@type': 'WebPage',
                '@id': pageId,
                url: schemaEntry.url,
                name: seoTitle ?: heading,
                headline: heading,
                description: toolSummary,
                inLanguage: language,
                isPartOf: { '@id': websiteId },
                about: { '@id': personId },
                mainEntity: { '@id': serviceId },
                image: shareImage,
                breadcrumb: { '@id': pageId ~ '-breadcrumb' },
                dateModified: schemaEntry.dateUpdated ? schemaEntry.dateUpdated|atom : null,
            }|filter(v => v)],
            Service: [{
                '@type': 'Service',
                '@id': serviceId,
                name: heading,
                description: toolSummary,
                serviceType: 'Homepage analysis',
                provider: { '@id': personId },
                areaServed: 'Worldwide',
                url: schemaEntry.url,
                offers: {
                    '@type': 'Offer',
                    price: '0',
                    priceCurrency: 'GBP',
                    availability: 'https://schema.org/InStock',
                    url: schemaEntry.url,
                },
            }|filter(v => v)],
            BreadcrumbList: [{
                '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
                '@id': pageId ~ '-breadcrumb',
                itemListElement: crumbs|merge([
                    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: schemaEntry.title|trim, item: schemaEntry.url },
                ]),
            }],
        } %}

    {#  ————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
        HOME — /
        ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————— #}
    {% else %}
        {# ProfilePage, not WebPage: this page's subject IS the person, which is
           what the type means and what `mainEntity` then confirms.

           The h1 is the same fallback the view uses — the single is titled "Home",
           a CMS label rather than a headline, so the TITLE is never the name. Over
           that sits the SEO tab, read the way the head reads it: Custom Page Title,
           else the h1 (which stays as `headline`); Meta Description, else the
           site's strapline. The share image is the Open Graph one at the 1200×630
           the og:image tag already rendered, with the site-wide fallback. #}
        {% set heading = (schemaEntry.longTitle ?? '')|trim ?: 'Ask me any question' %}
        {% set seoTitle = (schemaEntry.seoCustomPageTitle ?? '')|trim %}
        {% set shareAsset = craft.frontend.socialImage(schemaEntry) %}
        {% set shareImage = shareAsset ? {
            '@type': 'ImageObject',
            url: craft.frontend.socialImageUrl(shareAsset),
            width: 1200,
            height: 630,
            caption: (shareAsset.alt ?? '')|trim,
        }|filter(v => v) : null %}

        {% set schema = {
            ProfilePage: [{
                '@type': 'ProfilePage',
                '@id': pageId,
                url: homeUrl,
                name: seoTitle ?: heading,
                headline: heading,
                description: (schemaEntry.seoMetaDescription ?? '')|trim ?: strapline,
                inLanguage: language,
                isPartOf: { '@id': websiteId },
                mainEntity: { '@id': personId },
                image: shareImage,
                dateModified: schemaEntry.dateUpdated ? schemaEntry.dateUpdated|atom : null,
            }|filter(v => v)],
        } %}
    {% endif %}

    {# The two nodes every page carries. Merged last so the page's own type leads
       the markup, which is the order someone reading view-source wants. #}
    {% set schema = schema|merge({
        WebSite: [{
            '@type': 'WebSite',
            '@id': websiteId,
            url: homeUrl,
            name: siteName,
            description: strapline,
            inLanguage: language,
            publisher: { '@id': personId },
        }|filter(v => v)],
        Person: [{
            '@type': 'Person',
            '@id': personId,
            name: siteName,
            url: homeUrl,
            description: strapline,
            jobTitle: jobTitle,
            image: portraitImage,
            knowsAbout: knowsAbout,
            email: email,
            sameAs: sameAs,
        }|filter(v => v)],
    }) %}

    {# One block per type. A single node is emitted as itself with @context in
       front; several of a type get wrapped in a @graph, since a JSON-LD document
       is one node or a graph of them, never a bare array. A type whose list is
       empty (Review, on a study with no quotes) gets no block at all.

       JSON_HEX_TAG so a stray `</script>` in any field is encoded rather than
       closing the block early; JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE so accented client names
       stay readable instead of turning into \uXXXX. #}
    {% for type, nodes in schema|filter(nodes => nodes|length) %}
        <script type="application/ld+json" data-schema="{{ type }}">
            {{- (nodes|length > 1
                ? { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@graph': nodes }
                : { '@context': 'https://schema.org' }|merge(nodes|first))
                |json_encode(constant('JSON_HEX_TAG') b-or constant('JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE'))|raw -}}
        </script>
    {% endfor %}
{% endif %}
{# LEGACY SERVICE WORKER CLEANUP. This site used to install a caching service
   worker. It doesn't any more, and a stale one keeps serving its own cached copies
   of pages until it is removed — so this unregisters whatever is installed and then
   empties the caches it left behind.

   THE PAGE DELETES THE CACHES ITSELF rather than asking the worker to. The previous
   version messaged the controller with {command: 'removeCaches'} and relied on it
   having a listener for that — which the workers most in need of clearing, the ones
   that predate the handler, do not have. There was no way to find out, either: a
   message to a worker that ignores it fails silently. It was also one-shot, because
   after the first unregister there is no controller left to message. `caches` is on
   `window` in any secure context, so none of that indirection buys anything.

   UNREGISTERING IS NOT ENOUGH ON ITS OWN. CacheStorage is scoped to the ORIGIN, not
   to the registration, so the caches outlive the worker that wrote them. Dropping
   the registration stops stale pages being served; it leaves the bytes behind until
   the browser evicts them under quota. Both halves are needed, and they are
   independent — hence two blocks rather than one nested in the other. #}
<script type="text/javascript">
    if ('serviceWorker' in navigator) {
        navigator.serviceWorker.getRegistrations().then(function (registrations) {
            registrations.forEach(function (registration) {
                registration.unregister();
            });
        });
    }
    if ('caches' in window) {
        caches.keys().then(function (keys) {
            keys.forEach(function (key) {
                caches.delete(key);
            });
        });
    }
</script>